# dirvec, session 1: corpus + ground truth

Working name: dirvec (directory-level semantic representations for heterogeneous file trees). Rename freely.

## Claim under test
Directory representations built by pooling child embeddings collapse toward the dominant modality (text) and lose image-heavy children. Per-modality representatives (centroids, or k cluster reps per modality) do not. Measured at directory granularity on a real mixed-media tree.

Nothing published tests this at container level. RAPTOR / adRAP / EraRAG: text-only, chunked corpora, hierarchy is text summarization. ColPali, ColNomic, Cohere Embed 4, voyage-multimodal, MMDocIR: page or document level, no aggregation above the document. VectorVFS: vectors in file xattrs, no container layer, search walks the whole tree. "Directory-Aware Query and Maintenance in Vector Databases": directories as path filters, not semantic objects. Re-verify every one of these before citing.

## Session 1 goal
Corpus chosen and downloaded, manifest built, ground-truth builder written and run. No embeddings, no encoder choice, no retrieval code, no paper text. No GPU needed.

## Corpus requirements
- Real tree, not synthetic. Directories that mix photos, scanned PDFs, text PDFs, text, spreadsheets.
- Target: 2k to 5k files, >= 300 directories with >= 3 files each, image fraction spread across directories.
- Redistributable. Record source URL and licence per file. Case files are the right shape and the wrong licence; use public records.
- Candidates, in order:
  1. NTSB investigation dockets. Per-case folders: photos, factual reports, PDFs, spreadsheets. NTSB's own material is not subject to US copyright (17 USC 105); third-party submissions inside a docket may be, so note provenance per file.
  2. EPA Superfund site document repositories. PDF-heavy, some photos and maps.
  3. Any open-government release with per-case folders.
- Check terms before bulk download. Rate-limit. Store only what the study needs.

## Manifest
`data/manifest.jsonl`, one row per file: path, dir, modality, bytes, sha256, pages (PDF only), source_url, licence_note.
modality in {image, pdf_text, pdf_scanned, text, table, other}. PDF split: pdftotext chars per page below threshold (start at 50) => pdf_scanned.
`data/dirs.jsonl`, one row per directory: n_files, count per modality, image_frac, pdf_scanned_frac, depth.

## Ground truth (structural, leave-one-out)
For each file f in directory D with n(D) >= 3: query = f, relevant = {D}. The directory representation scored against that query must be built excluding f. Metric later: directory-stage recall@k, k in {1, 3, 5, 10}, reported per image_frac bucket [0,.2) [.2,.5) [.5,.8) [.8,1].

Dedupe first: exact by sha256; near-dup by perceptual hash (images) and simhash (text). When a near-dup sits outside f's directory, drop it. Without this, leave-one-out leaks.

Output: `data/gt_structural.jsonl` (query_path, relevant_dir, modality, image_frac_bucket).
Synthetic natural-language queries (LLM-generated per file) are session 2, if at all.

## Kill criterion
Stop and report if no candidate yields >= 200 qualifying directories with >= 40 per image_frac bucket. Corpus dies here for free.

## Deliverables
- `data/manifest.jsonl`, `data/dirs.jsonl`, `data/gt_structural.jsonl`
- `scripts/fetch.py`, `scripts/manifest.py`, `scripts/build_gt.py`
- `NOTES.md`: source, licence, counts per bucket, dedupe stats, go/no-go, open problems.
- Append to this file the exact prompt for session 2.

## Rules
- Ask Ali only at walls: credentials, terms that need a human click, licence doubt.
- One commit per deliverable. Nothing beyond what is listed.
- No em dashes anywhere in the repo.

## Later sessions, context only
One unified multimodal encoder for all files, so modality is not confounded by model choice. Two-stage retrieval: rank directories, then files inside the top directories. Ablation: pooled centroid vs per-modality centroids vs per-modality k-reps. Storage (xattrs, shadow index file per directory, overlay that ignores directory boundaries) is engineering, not the claim.
