# dirvec, session 3: pre-registered retest on fresh directories

Session 2 killed the claim as stated (see `results/session2.md`, NOTES.md session 2). On image
queries in the image-heavy buckets the pooled centroid was not beaten. The same table showed a
different pattern, found after the fact: the pooled centroid loses the minority modality of each
directory (image queries in text-heavy directories, text queries in image-heavy ones), and the
file-level baseline d was never beaten. This session tests the post hoc pattern on directories it
was not found on, with the hypothesis and kill criterion fixed before any number is computed.
No paper text. No storage engineering. No synthetic queries. No new encoder.

## Hypothesis (fixed now, do not edit after the first eval run)
H: a pooled centroid (a) loses queries whose modality is the minority in their directory;
per-modality k-reps (c) recover them.
Primary cells, recall@5, leave-one-out, same method as session 2:
  1. image queries in [0,.2) and [.2,.5) directories
  2. text-like queries (text, table, pdf_text, other) in [.5,.8) and [.8,1] directories
Kill: if the paired 95% bootstrap interval (1000 resamples over directories) of c - a does not
exclude zero in both pooled primary cells, H is dead. Report it plainly either way.
Secondary, reported, no kill: b - a in the same cells; d - c everywhere; vectors per directory
for a, b, c, d.

## Steps, in order
1. Fresh directories. Extend `scripts/fetch.py select` with an option to exclude records
   already in `data/selection.jsonl` and to write a second selection file
   (`data/selection_s3.jsonl`, seed 20261001, 100 per bucket from `data/pool.jsonl.gz`).
   Download into `data/corpus_s3/`. Same caps, same licences, same provenance fields.
   Throughput in session 2 was about 35 files/min from Zenodo; budget about 2 hours.
2. Manifest, dirs and ground truth for the new set: `manifest.py` and `build_gt.py` with an
   option for corpus and output paths (`data/manifest_s3.jsonl`, `data/dirs_s3.jsonl`,
   `data/gt_structural_s3.jsonl`). Before running, fix two session 2 findings in both scripts:
   `phash_file` must use the `embed.load_rgb` decode path (35 images were never
   dedupe-checked in session 1), and `note` must not contain absolute paths. Rerun session 1's
   `build_gt.py` too and report how many session 1 queries change; do not rewrite the
   session 2 results.
3. Kill criterion for the corpus, as in session 1: >= 40 directories per bucket, and at least
   150 queries in each pooled primary cell. If not met, stop and report.
4. Embed with `scripts/embed.py all --model jina-embeddings-v4 --text-mode query` into
   `data/emb/jina-embeddings-v4_s3/` (or teach it a manifest path). Same revision, same input
   rules, same venv (transformers 4.52.4, peft 0.15.2).
5. `scripts/eval.py` with a manifest option and the two primary cells as their own rows plus
   the paired c - a and b - a intervals. Rank only the new directories.
6. `results/session3.md`: the hypothesis block copied verbatim, the primary cells, the
   secondary table, the verdict. NOTES.md session 3 section. Append the session 4 prompt here.

## Inputs
- Repo at HEAD. Session 1 corpus rebuilt with `python scripts/fetch.py download --workers 20`.
- Model weights: `jinaai/jina-embeddings-v4` revision 853c867b65b749f3c3c72a06868140d842e04f06,
  public, no login. Qwen Research License: research use only.
- GPU with at least 12 GB. If the machine has none, say so before starting.

## Rules
- Ask Ali only at walls: GPU access, model weights that need a login, licence doubt.
- One commit per deliverable. Nothing beyond what is listed.
- No em dashes in anything you write. Session 1 data holds 17 em dashes inside verbatim
  Zenodo file keys and titles (NOTES.md session 2, open problem 5); leave them unless Ali decides.
