# dirvec, session 5: scale

Session 4 put a cost axis on the ablation (see `results/session4.md`, NOTES.md session 4). On 400
directories, two-stage retrieval with per-modality k-reps (c, k = 10) comes within 0.002 of the flat
walk d on file recall while scoring half the vectors; nothing beats d; a two-stage index is larger
than the flat one. At 400 directories every method answers in under 1 ms, so the cost argument is
untested where it would matter. This session repeats the comparison on every remaining eligible
directory of the pool, with an approximate index as the flat baseline.
No paper text. No new encoder. No synthetic queries.

## Hypothesis (fixed now, do not edit after the first eval run)
H5: on the large set, two-stage retrieval with c and k = 10 loses no more than 0.01 hit@10 against
the exact flat walk d, and scores at most half the vectors.
Kill: if the paired 95% bootstrap interval (1000 resamples over directories) of hit@10, two-stage
c at k = 10 minus exact d, lies entirely below -0.01 over all queries, H5 is dead. Report it
plainly either way.
Secondary, reported, no kill: the same for a and b; the session 3 primary cells (c - a, recall@5)
on the large set; an approximate flat baseline (HNSW over all file vectors) with its recall
against exact d, its index size and its query time; two-stage with an HNSW over the
representatives as stage 1.

## Steps, in order
1. Selection: `scripts/fetch.py select` with `--exclude data/selection.jsonl --exclude
   data/selection_s3.jsonl`, all remaining eligible records of `data/pool.jsonl.gz`, no cap per
   bucket (about 2,800 records expected; add the option if `--per-bucket` cannot express it),
   written to `data/selection_s5.jsonl`. Same eligibility, caps and licences. Report the counts
   per bucket; the set is not balanced and the tables must say so.
2. Download into `data/corpus_s5/`. Budget: about 34,000 files at Zenodo's guest limit of about
   133 files/min is more than 4 hours. Start it first and as one job.
3. Manifest, dirs and ground truth (`manifest_s5.jsonl`, `dirs_s5.jsonl`, `gt_structural_s5.jsonl`).
4. Embed with `scripts/embed.py all --manifest data/manifest_s5.jsonl --tag _s5`, new .xls rule.
   Before the run, make `embed.py` write its cache every 1,000 files, so a crash late in the pass
   does not lose it. One embedding job on the GPU at a time. Budget about 4 hours.
5. `scripts/twostage.py` on the large set. If the per-query leave-one-out k-means is too slow at
   this size, sample 5,000 queries with a fixed seed, stratified by bucket, and say so.
6. HNSW baselines (hnswlib or faiss, record version and parameters): over the file vectors (flat)
   and over the representatives (stage 1). Recall against exact search, index bytes, query time,
   one thread.
7. `results/session5.md`: the hypothesis block copied verbatim, the tables, the verdict. NOTES.md
   session 5 section. Append the session 6 prompt here.

## Inputs
- Repo at HEAD. The session 1 and session 3 sets are not needed unless a number is to be compared
  on them; if so, rebuild as in NOTES.md session 4.
- venv and weights as in NOTES.md session 4 (set HF_HUB_ENABLE_HF_TRANSFER=0 for the download,
  then HF_HOME=/workspace/hf and HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1).
- GPU with at least 12 GB, about 30 GB of disk, and a pod that lives at least 10 hours.

