# dirvec, session 9: the compact folder representation (replaces the proposed session 9)

The proposed session 9 (centering at file level against the flat baselines) is dropped: it retests
a published result (Li et al., NeurIPS 2025 workshop) on our data and cannot change the paper.
A prior-art search on 2026-09-30 (reports/prior_art_2026-09-30.md, to be added to the repo) found
no published work on pooled embeddings of mixed-media folders, found the multi-representative
remedy to be old (PinnerSage, MIND, MaxMF), and found the sharpest reviewer objection to be that
the minority-modality loss is plain count dilution rather than a modality effect.
This session answers the open question a solution needs answered: what is the most compact folder
representation that keeps the minority modality without losing the majority, on a frozen encoder
with post hoc processing only, and can one vector per folder in the encoder's own space ever do it.
Same set, same cache as sessions 5 to 8. No download, no GPU. No new encoder. No paper text.

## Definitions (fixed now)
- Input groups, calibration split (seed 20260930, 20 percent of directories, stratified), group
  means mu_img and mu_txt, centering, and the evaluation split exactly as in session 8. All 2,808
  directories are ranking candidates throughout.
- Dev queries: the 4,388 queries whose directory is in the calibration split (they were unused in
  session 8). Test queries: the 18,742 queries of the evaluation split, as in session 8. Every
  parameter below is chosen on dev only; the test split is evaluated once, after the choice, and
  nothing is changed after that run.
- Cells: all queries; P1 (image queries in [0,.2)+[.2,.5)); P2 (text-like queries in
  [.5,.8)+[.8,1]); M1 (image queries in [.5,.8)+[.8,1]); M2 (text-like queries in [0,.2)+[.2,.5)).
  Metric: recall@5, leave-one-out directory recall as in sessions 2 to 8; recall@1 and recall@10
  reported alongside. Paired 95 percent bootstrap intervals, 1000 resamples over directories.
- Reference rows on both splits: a, b, c, d (as before) and ac, cc, dc (centered, session 8).
- Families of candidate representations, all leave-one-out:
  F1, single vector in the encoder's space, weighted pooling: the folder vector is the unit
     normalised sum over its two input groups of w_g * mean_g, with w_g = n_g^alpha and alpha in
     {0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1}, on uncentered vectors (alpha = 1 is a, alpha = 0 is ab) and on
     centered vectors (alpha = 1 is ac, alpha = 0 is acb).
  F2, single vector after linear cross-modal alignment fitted on the calibration split, then the
     same weighted pooling grid on the aligned centered vectors. Three alignments, applied to every
     image-input vector (files and queries): (i) orthogonal Procrustes W fitted on the pairs
     (centered image-group mean, centered text-group mean) of every calibration directory that has
     at least one file in each group; (ii) CORAL: whiten the image-group residuals with the
     calibration image covariance and recolour with the calibration text covariance, both
     shrunk toward the identity with the Ledoit-Wolf estimate; (iii) ridge least squares on the
     same pairs as (i), lambda chosen on dev from {1, 10, 100, 1000}. Report the gap and the
     same-folder cosines (as in session 8) after each alignment.
  F3, one centroid per input group, at most two vectors per folder: b2 (uncentered) and bc2
     (centered), scored by max cosine like b. Also bc (the existing six-label b on centered
     vectors) for reference.
  F4, the partitioned vector: one index entry per folder of dimension 2d, [mu_img,c ; mu_txt,c],
     the centered group means (zeros for an absent group). The query of group g is placed as
     [w_same * q_c ; w_cross * q_c] with the same slot first for an image query and second for a
     text query; the score is the single inner product. w_same = 1; w_cross chosen on dev from
     {0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1}. Variant F4b adds a bias per (query group, folder group) pair chosen
     on dev as the mean score difference of same-group and cross-group matches on dev. Report the
     storage as two d-dimensional vectors and the query cost as one inner product.
  F5, the dilution control, type-blind k-means with max scoring, uncentered and centered, at
     three budgets: k = min(2, n); k = number of input groups present; k = the sum over the six
     modality labels of min(3, n_m), which is c's budget. Report cluster purity: over folders with
     both input groups, the share of files whose cluster's majority input group is their own.
- Selection on dev, per family: the configuration that maximises the minimum over the four cells
  (P1, P2, M1, M2) of (x minus c) in recall@5. Ties go to fewer vectors, then smaller alpha or
  w_cross.

## Hypotheses (fixed now, do not edit after the first test-split run)
H9a (no single vector suffices): no configuration of F1 or F2, after selection on dev, is within
0.02 recall@5 of c in both primary cells on the test split while also within 0.02 of c in both
majority cells. Kill: H9a is dead if the selected F1 or F2 configuration has every one of the four
paired intervals of (x minus c) with an upper bound above -0.02, that is, if no cell is
significantly more than 0.02 below c.
H9b (two centered group vectors suffice): the selected F3 or F4 configuration is within 0.02
recall@5 of c over all test queries and in every one of the four cells. Kill: H9b is dead if in any
of the five cells the paired 95 percent interval of (x minus c) lies entirely below -0.02.
H9c (modality, not dilution): c beats type-blind k-means at c's budget in both primary cells on the
test split. Kill: H9c is dead if the paired interval of (c minus F5 at c's budget) includes zero in
either primary cell.
Secondary, reported, no kill: every family's selected configuration against d and against dc in
all cells; the full dev grids; the F1 and F2 frontier (the four cells as alpha moves from 0 to 1);
purity of the type-blind clusters; vectors per folder for every row; two-stage hit@1 and hit@10
at k = 10 (twostage.py, exact, evaluation-split queries) for the selected F3 and F4 rows against
c and d.

## Steps, in order
1. Check git status (clean at the commit that adds this section). Add the dev and test query
   selection, the families and the selection rule to scripts/eval.py as options, defaults unchanged;
   add F3 and F4 stage 1 to scripts/twostage.py. Check that a, b, c, d, ac, cc, dc on the
   evaluation split reproduce results/session8.md before reading any new number.
2. Run the dev grids for F1 to F5, choose by the selection rule, record the choices in
   results/session9.md before the test run.
3. Run the test split once for the reference rows and the selected configurations, then the
   two-stage rows.
4. results/session9.md: the hypothesis block copied verbatim, the dev choices, the tables, the
   verdicts for H9a, H9b and H9c, and a plain statement of what the most compact representation
   that matches c is, with its vectors per folder and its loss in every cell. NOTES.md session 9
   section. Append a proposed session 10 prompt here.

## Inputs
- Repo at HEAD. On the /workspace volume: the cache data/emb/jina-embeddings-v4_s5/ (27,545 rows,
  gitignored), venv /workspace/venv.
- CPU only. On a pod whose cgroup allows fewer CPUs than nproc reports, set DIRVEC_THREADS,
  OMP_NUM_THREADS and OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS to the allowed count. Dev grids can run in parallel
  processes; keep the total at the allowed count.

