# dirvec, session 13: correction rerun of the session 9 single-vector rows

On 2026-10-01 the draft was audited against results/ by checkers that had not seen it
(reports/number_audit_2026-10-01.md). The audit found an evaluation bug that touches one family of
rows and nothing else: every F1 and F2 row of session 9 is scored too low.

- Mechanism: `build()` for the weighted-pooling base ("p", every `<space>_p<alpha>` name) multiplied a
  float32 mean by a NumPy float64 scalar (`g.sum() ** param`), so the folder vector was float64.
  `rank_rep()` computed the query's own-directory score in float64, wrote it into a float32 score
  row, and counted `s > own` against the unrounded float64 value. Whenever the float32 rounding went
  up, the query's own directory counted as scoring strictly higher than itself: rank + 1 in about
  half the queries.
- Evidence in the committed tables: u_p1 is a and c_p1 is ac by definition (session 9, F1), yet
  results/session9.md prints recall@1 0.274 against 0.555 and 0.295 against 0.588, and recall@5
  0.681 against 0.689 and 0.715 against 0.724. Every F1 and F2 row has recall@1 between 0.27 and
  0.30. scripts/selftest_ranks.py reproduces this on a synthetic corpus with the old eval.py and
  passes with the fix (commit before this section).
- Not touched: every other row of the study is float32 on both sides of the comparison (a, b, c,
  c4, c2, d, ab, acb, ac, cc, dc, b2, bc, bc2, f4, f4b and the tb rows). Sessions 2 to 8 and 10 to
  12 never used the weighted-pooling builder. The rerun checks this (C2 below).
- Direction: the bias lowers the single-vector rows, so it favours H9a. The verdict has to be
  re-read on corrected rows before the paper quotes any of them.

Same set, same cache, same calibration split (seed 20260930) as session 9. CPU only. No new
representation, no new parameter, no paper text in this session.

## Definitions (fixed now)
- Everything as in session 9: input groups, the calibration split, dev queries (4,388) and test
  queries (18,742), the five cells, recall@5 with recall@1 and recall@10 alongside, paired 95 percent
  bootstrap intervals over directories, the families, and the selection rule (per family, the
  configuration that maximises the minimum over P1, P2, M1, M2 of x minus c on dev; ties to fewer
  vectors, then smaller alpha; F2 is selected on the two-fold cross-fitted dev grid, as in session 9).
- Runs (scripts/s13_run.sh, one chain, new tags so that no session 9 file is overwritten):
  1. dev grid, literal: the 66 names of session 9 plus ab and acb (`--tag _s13dev`);
  2. dev grid, two-fold cross-fitted F2: the 37 names of session 9 (`--tag _s13devcv`);
  3. test split: the 33 rows of session 9, ab and acb, and every other F2 name (59 rows,
     `--tag _s13test`), so that whatever configuration the corrected dev grids select is already
     scored. The test rows are all computed in one run; which of them carry the verdict is fixed by
     the dev grids and the selection rule, not by looking at the test table.
- Checks, read before any corrected number:
  C1. scripts/selftest_ranks.py passes on the pod.
  C2. Reproduction: for every row that does not use the weighted-pooling builder, the ranks in the
      new files are identical to the session 9 files (ranks_s9dev, ranks_s9devcv, ranks_s9test) on
      every query. If any such row differs, stop and report; nothing else is read.
  C3. Identity: in the new files u_p1 has a's ranks and c_p1 has ac's on at least 99 percent of the
      queries, and u_p0 and c_p0 match ab and acb the same way, with recall@5 within 0.001 in each
      of the five cells.

## Hypothesis (H9a as fixed in session 9, re-read on the corrected rows; nothing reworded)
H9a (no single vector suffices): no configuration of F1 or F2, after selection on dev, is within
0.02 recall@5 of c in both primary cells on the test split while also within 0.02 of c in both
majority cells. Kill: H9a is dead if the selected F1 or F2 configuration has every one of the four
paired intervals of (x minus c) with an upper bound above -0.02, that is, if no cell is
significantly more than 0.02 below c.
- The verdict is read on the configurations the corrected dev grids select. If they differ from
  session 9's selections (c_p0.5 and coral_p0.75), both sets are reported and the corrected
  selection carries the verdict.
- Expectation, written before the run, not a kill: the F1 and F2 rows rise by about 0.005 to 0.015
  recall@5 and by about a factor of two at recall@1; H9a survives; the best minimum over the four
  cells among the one-vector test rows moves from -0.060 to about -0.05.
Secondary, reported, no kill:
- for every one-vector test row: x minus c per cell with intervals, the minimum over the four cells,
  the number of cells with the point estimate within 0.02 of c, and the number of cells whose
  interval has an upper bound above -0.02. If any one-vector row, selected or not, has all four upper
  bounds above -0.02, say so plainly: H9a would still stand by its rule (selection is on dev), but
  the paper's sentence that no single vector matches c would have to name the exception;
- the count of one-vector rows in the dev grids and in the test run (session 9's prose says 22; its
  table has 18);
- the in-sample and the cross-fitted recall@5 of Procrustes at alpha 0.5 in P1 on dev (the leak
  illustration; session 9's prose quotes a smoke-test figure);
- the equal-weight corners (u_p0 and c_p0) against a and against c;
- how far each affected row moved (scripts/s13_check.py).
H9b and H9c and every F3, F4 and F5 number of session 9 stand as they are if C2 holds.

