# dirvec, session 16: the target type known in advance (type composition as a filter, as a prior, and type-only groups)

Why this session exists. Ali asked on 2026-10-02 at 00:03 Toronto: when the searcher already knows
the target type (a video, an image, a PDF), does storing each folder's type composition in its
metadata help? Every result so far scores a query against folders without using its type. This
session measures three ways a system could use a known type, on the confirmation set S11 under all
four encoders, from the caches already on the volume: CPU only, no new embedding. It also answers
the question the remedy invites: if the type is known, why not keep one pooled vector per type and
score the query against that one? The corpus was selected for mixed folders (images in 96.8
percent of S11 folders by the manifest, both input groups in 95.5 percent), so a filter by type can
prune little here, and this corpus cannot show what a tree of single-type folders would gain. Ali
asked for the session on 2026-10-02 at 00:08 Toronto. Target: the SIGIR version, not the preprint.

## Definitions (fixed now)
- Set and queries: S11, the session 11 evaluation split (calibration fraction 0.2, seed 20261102),
  the evaluation queries with a vector, all directories ranked. Dev queries (those whose directory
  is in the calibration split) choose the prior's weight and nothing else.
- Encoders: E1 to E4, the S11 caches data/emb/jina-embeddings-v4_s11, jina-clip-v2_s11,
  gme-Qwen2-VL-2B-Instruct_s11 and nomic-embed-v1.5_s11, uncentered unit vectors as in rows a, c
  and d. Nothing is embedded.
- The type a searcher can know is the file's kind as a file system records it, from the study's
  labels: image; pdf (pdf_text or pdf_scanned, since a searcher who wants a PDF does not know
  whether it was scanned); text; table; other. A query's type is its own kind.
- Composition: per directory, the number of its files of each kind over the manifest (every kept
  file, with or without a vector). The query file is counted in its own directory's composition,
  since the stored folder holds it; leave-one-out applies to the vectors only. share_T(D) is the
  directory's files of kind T over all its files.
- Base rows: a (pooled centroid), c (per-modality k-means, k = min(3, n_m) per label) and d (every
  file), eval.py's builders, leave-one-out as in eval.py. Their ranks must equal the ranks files of
  sessions 11, 14 and 15 (rank_a, rank_c, rank_d of ranks_s11_e1, ranks_s11_e2, ranks_s14_e3 and
  ranks_s15_e4) query for query; any difference stops the run before any other number is printed.
- x+F, the filter, for x in a, c, d: only directories whose composition holds the query's kind are
  ranked. The query's own directory always is.
- x+P, the prior: score(q, D) + tau * ln share_T(D) over the directories x+F ranks. tau per encoder
  and per x, chosen on the dev queries: the value in {0.001, 0.002, 0.005, 0.01, 0.02, 0.05, 0.1,
  0.2, 0.5} with the highest recall@5 over all dev queries, ties to the smaller tau. As tau goes to
  zero, x+P becomes x+F.
- x_T, type-only groups (Ali's first design, separate embedding groups per type, with the type
  known): a directory is scored only by its representatives of the query's kind. a_T: the unit
  mean of its files of that kind, one vector per kind present. c_T: c's representatives of the
  labels of that kind (a pdf query sees the pdf_text and the pdf_scanned representatives). d_T: its
  files of that kind. A directory with no vector of that kind is not ranked. Leave-one-out as in
  eval.py: the own directory's groups are rebuilt without the query file.
- Singletons: queries whose own directory keeps no other file of their kind with a vector. Under
  x_T the own directory has nothing to match, so they are misses by construction. They are counted
  per cell and reported apart. Every comparison with an x_T row is on the other queries (the
  non-singleton set), with x, x+F and x+P on the same set beside it; the singletons get x, x+F and
  x+P only.
- Cells: all, P1, P2, M1, M2 as in session 9; also the query's fine label (image, pdf_text,
  pdf_scanned, text, table, other) and its input group (image inputs: image and pdf_scanned; text
  inputs: the rest). Metric recall@5, with recall@1 and recall@10 alongside. Paired 95 percent
  bootstrap intervals, 1000 resamples of the cell's directories, eval.py's cell seeds (all 1, P1 50,
  P2 51, M1 56, M2 57, the fine labels 20 plus their position in eval.py's MODALITIES, the input
  groups 90 and 91), the same seeds on the non-singleton set. Rank as eval.py: 1 plus the number of
  ranked directories that score strictly higher than the own directory (float32 scores, float64 once
  the prior is added).
- Code: scripts/typeq.py, one run per encoder, and the driver scripts/s16_run.sh.

## Hypotheses (fixed now, do not edit after the first evaluation run)
H16a (knowing the type does not rescue the pooled vector): on the evaluation queries, the paired 95
percent interval of (c+F minus a+F) in recall@5 lies above zero in P1 and in P2. Read under each
encoder. Kill: H16a is dead under an encoder if either interval includes zero or lies below it.
H16b (Ali's design: with the type known, one pooled vector per type keeps per-type
representatives): on the non-singleton evaluation queries, no paired 95 percent interval of (a_T
minus c_T) in recall@5 over all queries, P1, P2, M1 or M2 lies entirely below -0.02. Read under
each encoder. Kill: H16b is dead under an encoder if any of the five does.
H16c (a composition prior trades the minority for the majority): with tau chosen on the dev
queries, the paired 95 percent interval of (c+P minus c+F) in recall@5 lies below zero in P1 and in
P2. Read under each encoder. Kill: H16c is dead under an encoder if either interval includes zero
or lies above it.
The paper states a hypothesis as holding only where it holds under all four encoders.
Secondary, reported, no kill: every row in every cell with recall@1 and recall@10; x+F minus x,
x+P minus x+F, x_T minus x+F, c_T minus d_T, a_T minus d_T and a_T minus c+F with intervals; the
dev grid and the selected tau per encoder and row; c+P on the evaluation queries for every tau
(descriptive, nothing is chosen on it); per kind, the directories holding it, the directories with a
vector of it, the queries, the singletons and the mean number of directories x+F ranks; singletons
per cell; vectors stored per directory (a_T: the kinds present with a vector).
Expectation, written before the run, not a kill: the filter changes little for image and text
queries, which nearly every folder holds, and more for pdf, table and other queries; H16a survives
under all four encoders; H16b dies in M2 under most encoders (one vector is too coarse for a
folder's many texts, as bc2 was); c+P gains in M1 and M2 and loses in P1 and P2.

## Steps, in order
1. Commit this brief, scripts/typeq.py and scripts/s16_run.sh before anything runs. typeq.py
   passed a CPU test in the workspace on synthetic vectors in the S11 manifest's order: its a, c and
   d ranks equal eval.py's on the same synthetic cache query for query, and its F, P and T ranks
   equal a plain-loop recomputation on a sample (`--selftest`, which the pod run repeats on 200
