# dirvec, session 20: per-modality representatives in a metadata field of fixed size

Why this session exists. A folder vector is for a folder's metadata: it is read before any file in
the folder, and the field holds a fixed number of bytes. Ali, 2 October 2026, 18:28 and 18:33
Toronto: the only reason for a folder vector is space in metadata fields and that it can be reached
before the files; the idea is metadata allocations in file systems that take up to 64 kilobits, on
the modern operating systems where consumer-grade models run. The draft counts c in vectors (about
five per folder), and at the encoders' float32 sizes five vectors are 15 to 40 KiB. This session
counts bytes: it stores every representative in five formats, from float32 down to one bit per
dimension, and asks whether c in a few KiB still does what it does in float32. CPU only, on the S11
caches already on the volume; no new embedding. Numbered 20 because 19 is the proposed GitHub second
source (NOTES.md), not registered.

## Definitions (fixed now)
- Set, queries, encoders: S11, the session 11 evaluation queries (calibration seed 20261102), E1 to
  E4 with their S11 caches; leave-one-out as eval.py; the cells all, P1, P2, M1 and M2 with
  eval.py's seeds (1, 50, 51, 56, 57).
- Formats (scripts/quant.py), per stored vector: f32 (4 bytes per dimension, the vector unchanged),
  f16 (2), int8 (1 byte per dimension: round(127 x / max|x|) per vector, the scale not stored), int4
  (half a byte: round(7 x / max|x|)), bin (one bit: +1 where x >= 0, else -1). Every format but f32
  is decoded to float32 and unit-normalised; the query stays float32 (it is embedded at search time)
  and a folder's score is the largest cosine over its stored representatives.
- Rows: a_f, c_f and d_f for each format f: eval.py's a, c and d with every representative stored in
  f, the own folder rebuilt without the query and then stored. d_f32 is eval.py's d, the reference:
  every file of every folder opened.
- Bytes per folder: representatives times bytes per vector, over the 2,597 folders ranked, from all
  their embedded files. Dimensions E1 2,048, E2 1,024, E3 1,536, E4 768, so one vector is 8,192,
  4,096, 6,144 and 3,072 bytes in f32 and 256, 128, 192 and 96 in bin. From the manifest (before
  skipped files) c has 5.28 representatives per folder on average, 9 at the 95th percentile and 14
  at most; d has 10.0 files on average and 60 at most.
- Budgets: 2, 4, 8, 16 and 64 KiB per folder. 8 KiB is the 64 kilobits Ali named; 64 KiB is
  Linux's ceiling for one extended attribute value; 4 KiB is one ext4 block, which with the inode's
  spare space holds all of a file's extended attributes unless the ea_inode feature is on; 2 KiB is
  S3's allowance for user metadata. For each budget the outputs name the most precise format in which
  every folder fits, for a, c and d.
- Reproduction check: the f32 ranks of a, c and d must equal eval.py's in the session 11 ranks files
  (ranks_s11_e1, ranks_s11_e2, ranks_s14_e3, ranks_s15_e4) for every query; otherwise exit 3, FAILED,
  no numbers. Self-test: 200 evaluation queries per encoder (default_rng(20)), every row recomputed in
  float64; a rank that differs and is not explained by scores within 1e-5 of the own folder's is a
  failure (exit 4).

## Hypotheses (fixed now, do not edit after the first evaluation)
H20a (one byte per dimension): under each of E1 to E4, no paired 95 percent interval of
(c_int8 - d_f32) in recall@5 over all queries, P1, P2, M1 or M2 lies entirely below -0.02 (H15b's
rule). Dead under an encoder if any of the five does.
H20b (one bit per dimension): the same for (c_bin - d_f32). Dead under an encoder if any of the five
intervals lies entirely below -0.02.
H20c (one bit per dimension still keeps the minority modality): under each encoder, the paired
interval of (c_bin - a_f32) in recall@5 lies above zero in P1 and in P2. Dead under an encoder if
either includes zero or lies below it.
What the paper says: where H20b survives, c fits in the bytes the bin row of the bytes table gives
and still routes within 0.02 of opening every folder; where only H20a survives, the claim is one
byte per dimension at the budget its row gives; where H20c survives, one bit per dimension of c
beats one float32 vector of a on the minority files.
Secondary, reported, no kill: every row at recall@1, 5 and 10 in every cell; the cost of each format
(c_f - c_f32, a_f - a_f32, d_f - d_f32); c_f - a_f and c_f - d_f at equal precision; the lower bounds
against -0.02 (the stricter non-inferiority reading); the bytes table (mean, 95th percentile, max,
share of folders within each budget) for a, c and d; the budget table.
Expectation, written before the run, not a kill: int8 costs nothing measurable (c_int8 within 0.005
of c_f32 in every cell); one bit costs more at 768 dimensions (E4) than at 2,048 (E1), and H20b may
fail in a minority cell under a dual tower; H20c survives under all four, since c - a was 0.16 to
0.50 in the minority cells.

## Steps, in order
1. Commit this brief, scripts/quant.py and scripts/s20_run.sh before anything runs. quant.py passed a
   CPU test in the workspace on synthetic vectors (96 dimensions) for a 300-folder subset of S11 in
   manifest order: its f32 a, c and d ranks equal eval.py's query for query (1,997 queries) and the
   float64 self-test passed on 150 of them. No session 20 number on a dirvec vector has been
   computed.
2. Check the RunPod balance. The Claude weekly limit was close to its end on the evening of
   2 October (it resets 4 October 06:00 UTC), so the run is one launch and the watcher stops the pod
   if this conversation is cut off. Resume bmao44n64ohrwp (MIG slice, $0.59 an hour, 3.4 vCPUs), run
   /workspace/setup_pod.sh, start scripts/pod_autostop.sh with a deadline 2 hours after the resume
   and the markers /workspace/logs/s20/DONE and /workspace/logs/s20/FAILED; verify its pid.
3. `git pull` in the pod's clone, then `bash scripts/s20_run.sh` as a background job: environment
   check, the four caches and ranks files present, quant.py for E1 to E4 in turn with the
   reproduction check and the self-test; it writes results/session20_outputs.md (outputs verbatim),
   commits and pushes it, and writes DONE.
4. Confirm the pod stopped. results/session20.md (this hypothesis block verbatim, the verdicts, the
   secondaries) and the NOTES.md section, each as its own commit.

## Inputs and budget
- On the /workspace volume: the four S11 caches and their session 11 ranks files, the E1 venv
  (numpy, scikit-learn). Nothing is downloaded.
- Expected: about 40 minutes (session 16 rebuilt c per query on the same pod; quant.py adds 15
  products per encoder), about $0.40. The watcher caps it at 2 hours ($1.18).

## Rules
- One commit per deliverable. No em dashes in anything written.
- Nothing is chosen on the evaluation queries; formats, budgets and rules are fixed above.
- If the reproduction check or the self-test fails, stop: FAILED, no verdicts. If the pod cannot be
  reached or a step fails, stop the pod and report; do not work around it.

