# dirvec, session 19: the minority-modality loss on a second source (directories of GitHub repositories)

On 4 October 2026 three reviewers read the SIGIR version blind and all three recommended reject
(reports/panel_2026-10-04.md). They named the same first fix: a pre-registered replication on a
second, structurally different source, with folders people built for their own work, real nesting,
folders not selected one by one, owner-clustered inference, a name-only baseline, and queries not
derived from folder membership. This session is that replication. Its design takes the conditions
the reviewers set when they were shown a first plan (same report): primary cells that keep a
same-modality sibling, an owner-weighted estimator, a smallest effect of interest, a strict margin
only where c is a real compression, source code reported apart, and a timestamp from outside the
repository. Everything below is fixed before any repository is listed.

## Definitions (fixed now)
- Frame. Days are drawn uniformly without replacement from 2016-01-01 to 2025-12-31 (a shuffle of
  all days, seed 20261104, read in that order). For a drawn day the pool holds every public
  repository created that day with at least five stars, as the GitHub search API returns it without
  a token; an interval with more than 1,000 results is halved until it has at most 1,000. A
  repository is usable when it is not a fork, has a default branch, is at most 200 MB by the API's
  size field, and carries one of these licences as GitHub reports it: MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD-2-Clause,
  BSD-3-Clause, ISC, Unlicense, CC0-1.0, CC-BY-4.0, 0BSD. Days are read until the pool holds 3,000
  usable repositories, and 2,500 more per round (at most six rounds) if the scored set is not full.
- Folder. A directory of a repository at any depth, with its direct children only, read from the
  tarball of the default branch (pinned by the commit id in the tarball). Directories under a path
  component that starts with a dot, or under node_modules, vendor, third_party, bower_components,
  site-packages, Pods or __pycache__, are not listed. Kept files: the extension table and the 25 MB
  cap of session 1 (scripts/fetch.py), not empty, name not starting with a dot. Source code is text
  under that table. A directory is eligible with 3 to 100 kept files and at most 100 MB. It is mixed
  when it keeps at least one file with an image extension and at least one other kept file.
- Scored set. Usable repositories are read in a seeded random order. Every eligible directory of a
  repository that is read is listed in data/ghdirs_s19.jsonl, drawn or not, and the share of mixed
  directories is reported from that list. From each repository the scored set takes at most two
  mixed and at most two other eligible directories, drawn at random with a seed fixed by the
  repository's id, until it holds 1,000 mixed and 600 other directories or 24,000 files. Mixed
  directories are over-sampled on purpose, and the natural rate is the one in the list.
- Ground truth, near-duplicate rules, input rules, rows, scoring: unchanged (build_gt.py, embed.py,
  eval.py). Rows a, ac, c, cc, d, dc, tb2c, tbc, and one new row cs: per-modality k-means with
  k = min(n, max(3, ceil(sqrt(n)))) per label. The eval.py command line is session 11's with those
  rows and calibration seed 20261104 (the 20 percent calibration directories give no file queries).
- Encoders. E1 decides every hypothesis. E4 and then E2 run if the balance allows and are read by
  the same rules, reported, with no verdict of their own. E3 is not run in this session.
- Cells. P1, P2, M1, M2 as in session 3. P1s and P2s are the queries of P1 and P2 whose folder
  still holds at least one other embedded file of the query's input group once the query is left
  out. A lone image among texts has no sibling to be found through under any row, so it cannot show
  what pooling loses. The full cells are reported beside them.
- Owner: the account that holds the repository, lowercased. Estimator of every registered test:
  the mean over owners of the owner's mean over its queries. Interval: percentile, 10,000 resamples
  of owners. A test with fewer than 100 owners is inconclusive.
- Reading of a loss x - y on an interval [L, U]: confirmed if L is at least +0.05; below +0.05 if U
  is under +0.05; present with the size open if L is above zero; inconclusive otherwise.
- Clean subset: both file-name routers of session 17 (max over names, pooled names) miss the folder
  at rank 5, and the folder holds no file with the query's stem and another extension.
- Kinds, for reporting only: image, code (py r jl m c h cpp hpp java js ts sh sql lean ipynb rmd
  qmd), config (json xml yaml yml toml ini cfg log cff bib), table, prose (every other text-like
  file). A no-code folder holds no code file.
- Commit-subject queries. From the history of each repository (the 3,000 newest commits reachable
  from the pinned commit, no merges, no author data): a commit qualifies for a scored directory
  when every path it changed lies directly in that directory and at least one is a kept file of it.
  The query is the subject line with every token removed that holds a slash or backslash, equals
  the name or stem of a kept file of the directory, or ends in an extension of the study's table.
  Dropped: subjects that start with "merge " or "revert ", that equal "add files via upload",
  "initial commit" or "first commit", or that keep fewer than four words of three or more letters;
  repeated texts within a directory. At most the three newest per directory. The relevant folder is
  that directory; representations use all of a directory's embedded files; all scored directories
  are ranked.

## Hypotheses (fixed now, do not edit after the first harvest)
Primary family, three tests, each read on its 98.33 percent interval (Bonferroni over the three):
- H19a. Under E1 on the evaluation queries, c minus a in P1s and in P2s. Confirmed if both cells
  are confirmed. Replicated with the size open if both are confirmed or present. Not replicated at
  the registered size if either cell is below +0.05. Inconclusive otherwise.
- H19c. Under E1, on the queries whose folder c does not hold whole (a label keeps more than three
  files once the query is left out), c minus d. Not inferior if L is at least -0.02; inferior if U
  is under -0.02; inconclusive otherwise.
Secondary, with a registered reading on the 95 percent interval:
- H19b. The H19a statistic on the clean subset of P1s and of P2s, read as a loss.
- H19d. With the commit subject as the query, c minus a on image-heavy directories (image fraction
  at least 0.5), read as a loss. Gate: file-level search d reaches recall@5 of at least 0.10 on
  those queries and more than the file names alone. If the gate fails the test is untestable.
- The H19a statistic on no-code folders; on all of P1 and of P2; cs minus d where c differs from d.
Reported without a reading: the directory bootstrap and the query-weighted owner-clustered
intervals of every difference; the file-name baseline; the cells by kind of the query file; by
folder size (3 to 10, 11 to 30, 31 to 100 embedded files), by the number of same-group siblings
left, by depth in the repository; c minus tbc (the H9c question on this source), tb2c minus c,
centering; the modality gap and same-folder cosines; recall@1, @3 and @10; the rate of mixed
directories by depth and size; E4 and E2 by the same rules and their loss against E1's.

## What each outcome will mean for the paper (fixed now)
- H19a confirmed or replicated: the loss holds on a second source whose folders, owners, images and
  texts differ from Zenodo's, with the sizes this session gives. The paper reports both sources.
- H19a not replicated at the registered size: the paper says so in the abstract, gives the Zenodo
  sizes as sizes on Zenodo records, and reports the registered breakdowns (siblings, kinds, names,
  d's level) as the place to look for the reason. No breakdown is promoted to a result.
- H19c inferior: three representatives per label are not enough for larger folders, and the paper
  says so and reports cs.
- Inconclusive is reported as inconclusive.

## Steps, in order
1. This brief, scripts/fetch_gh.py, scripts/s19.py, scripts/s19_run.sh and the self-test
   scripts/s19_selftest.py (synthetic repositories behind a local stand-in for the API; it runs the
   whole pipeline) in one commit. An OpenTimestamps proof of that commit's id in prereg/, committed
   next, before the harvest.
2. One run of scripts/s19_run.sh on a pod: pool, harvest, manifest, ground truth, history, the
   commit-subject query set, the corpus counts; the corpus files are pushed before any embedding.
   Then E1: embedding, eval.py, the commit-subject queries, s19.py validity. Then E4 and E2.
3. results/session19_outputs.md (verbatim, pushed by the pod), results/session19.md (hypotheses
   copied from here, verdicts, reading), NOTES.md with a list of deviations: what this brief said,
   what was done, why, which numbers had been seen.

## Inputs and budget
- RunPod balance $12.87 on 4 October 2026; nothing is bought. Expected $5 to $9.
- No GitHub token is needed. If the pod's address is refused by the search API, the pod's stored
  credential for this repository is used for the search requests only.

## Rules
- Ask Ali only at walls. One commit per deliverable. No em dashes.
- Nothing is selected on the new corpus. s19.py validity stops before any new number if it does not
  reproduce eval.py's printed intervals.
- A step that fails is fixed and resumed (S19_RESUME=1); a number seen before a fix is listed among
  the deviations.

## Amendments before the harvest (4 October 2026, night; no repository has been listed)
The run was read once more before it started. The definitions, cells, tests and readings above are
unchanged. Code only:
- scripts/fetch_gh.py. The file budget counts as reached when fewer than three files of it are left,
  since no eligible directory can then fit (before, a budget one or two files short was never "full"
  and the harvest would have read the rest of the pool for nothing). A rate limit of the search API
  is waited out up to 40 times a query instead of 12, and a tarball is tried six times instead of
  four. Any error while a tarball is read makes that repository a failed one instead of stopping the
  harvest. A directory whose files do not all come out of the tarball is left out and counted.
  Commit history is fetched without a stored credential and without a prompt.
- scripts/s19_run.sh. It stops if more than 10 percent of the repositories read gave no tarball
  (throttling or network), because the draw would then not be the registered one. Before anything
  is embedded it checks that every file of the manifest is on the disk, and rebuilds the corpus from
  the selection by commit id if not (a stopped pod loses its container disk, and embed.py would
  count a missing file as a skipped input). After the outputs are pushed it runs
  scripts/s19_followup.sh if a later commit has added one; a follow-up is registered in its own
  section of this file before it runs and cannot change a session 19 output.
- scripts/s19_preflight.sh (new). On the pod before the run: the tools, the self-test in the pod's
  own Python, and the real endpoints on a throwaway draw (seed 7, one day, nine directories, files
  with the suffix _p19, removed afterwards). It computes no retrieval number.
An OpenTimestamps proof of this commit's id is in prereg/session19_amend_commit.txt.ots.

