# dirvec, session 21: what to store in a fixed number of bytes of a folder's metadata (both sources)

Why this session exists. The reviewers of 4 October (reports/panel_2026-10-04.md) agreed on a second
objection after the second source: the loss compares one stored vector with about five, so pooling
is confounded with budget. They asked for the budget in bytes, for matched baselines at equal bytes
(sampled files, medoids, farthest-first, one mean per kind, every file at one bit when it fits), and
for a summary that can be maintained, which k-means representatives are not and a mean is. Session
20 stored a, c and d in five formats. This session fixes the bytes instead and asks which summary to
put in them. CPU only, on cached vectors: the S19 caches of the run now on the pod and the S11
caches on the volume.

When this was fixed. Committed on 5 October 2026 while the session 19 pod (started 01:52 UTC) was in
its harvest: no file of the new source had been embedded and no retrieval number of it existed.
Seen of the new source by then: the pool counts (7,959 repositories, 3,080 usable, 8 days), the
harvest counters of its first round (3,080 repositories read: 707 mixed and 600 other directories,
9,997 files) and of the start of its second (771 mixed), and the preflight's throwaway draw (seed 7:
12 of 81 eligible directories mixed, 77 files embedded by each encoder, no retrieval number). S11 is another matter: sessions 9, 13 and 20 scored blind k-means at
c's budget and a, c and d in five formats on it, so S11 is reported here as the earlier source and
no hypothesis is read on it.

## Definitions (fixed now)
- Sets and encoders. S19: session 19's manifest, ground truth, evaluation queries (calibration seed
  20261104) and caches; E1 decides, E4 and E2 are run if session 19 embedded them. S11: session 11's
  evaluation queries (calibration seed 20261102) under E1 to E4. Leave-one-out, scoring and ranks as
  eval.py: the float32 query against what the folder stores, the folder's score its largest cosine,
  the rank 1 plus the folders scoring strictly higher; the query's own folder is rebuilt without the
  query and then stored.
- Budgets: 512 bytes, 1, 2, 4 and 8 KiB per folder. A vector at one bit per dimension takes
  ceil(dim / 8) bytes (E1 256, E2 128, E3 192, E4 96), so a budget of B bytes holds
  n = B // ceil(dim / 8) of them. A folder with at most n embedded files is stored whole, all its
  files at one bit, by every policy but mean, kindmean and fsample.
- Policies (scripts/budget.py), each within B bytes:
  mean: the pooled mean (eval.py's a) in the most precise of f32, f16, int8, int4 and bin that fits
  (session 20's formats).
  kindmean: one mean per kind (eval.py's b), all in the most precise format in which they fit
  together; when one bit each does not fit, the kinds with the most files are kept.
  sample: n files at one bit. The n are dealt to the kinds present one at a time, the kind with the
  most files first (ties in eval.py's order of the labels); a kind keeps its files with the smallest
  hash priority, sha1 of "21:" and the file's manifest path. The sample is fixed by the file names,
  needs no clustering, and every stored vector is a file's own.
  fsample: the same with a fixed share: every kind present keeps its n // kinds files of smallest
  priority and unused slots stay empty (below one slot a kind, the n kinds with the most files keep
  one file each). It merges exactly whenever n is at least the number of kinds: a parent's fsample
  is then computed from its children's without opening a file.
  usample: the n files with the smallest hash priority, kinds ignored, one bit. It merges exactly:
  the bottom-n of a union is the bottom-n of the two bottom-n samples. sample, which refills unused
  slots, does not merge exactly.
  ffirst: n files by farthest-first traversal from the file nearest the mean, one bit.
  kmeans: min(n, files) centroids of spherical k-means over all files, one bit.
  medoid: for each of those clusters the member nearest its centroid, one bit; members within 1e-6
  of the nearest tie and the first in manifest order is taken.
  kkind: the allotment of sample; each kind's files reduced to its allotment by k-means, one bit.
  allbits: every file at one bit on its first m dimensions, m = min(dim, 8 (B // files)); the query
  is cut to the same m dimensions and renormalised. It relies on the leading dimensions carrying
  the most, which E1 and E2 were trained for and E3 was not; reported as measured.
  A kind is the manifest's modality label. k-means here is spherical, in kernel form on the folder's
  cosine matrix, seeded by farthest-first traversal, at most 25 Lloyd steps, no random number; it is
  not eval.py's k-means, so kkind at three per kind is not c, and c's ranks are taken from eval.py's
  ranks file as a reference row. sample and usample are run again under the hash seeds 22 to 25 and
  the spread over the five seeds is reported; the tests read seed 21.
- Cells. all: every evaluation query. minority: P1s and P2s of session 19 together, an image query
  in a text-heavy folder or a text-like query in an image-heavy folder that still has a sibling of
  its input group. Not whole, at a budget: the queries whose folder holds more than n embedded files
  besides the query, so that what is stored is a strict subset of the folder. P1s, P2s, M1 and M2
  beside them, and the folder sizes 3 to 10, 11 to 30 and 31 to 100 embedded files.
- Estimator on S19: session 19's, the mean over owners of the owner's mean, percentile intervals
  from 10,000 resamples of owners; a test with fewer than 100 owners is inconclusive. On S11:
  query-weighted with 1,000 resamples of the cell's directories, as eval.py.
- Checks before any number: the mean in f32 must give eval.py's rank_a and every file in f32 its
  rank_d for every query; a difference is accepted only where a folder scores within 1e-5 of the
  own folder, and is counted; otherwise exit 3 and no output. The registered run stops before any
  number if a directory row has no owner or the budgets are not the ones above.
  scripts/s21_selftest.py recomputes every policy at every budget from the vectors with a second,
  plain implementation (explicit centroids, loops) on a synthetic set with files and whole folders
  without vectors, and must pass on the pod before the runs.
- Read before the commit by a reviewer who had not seen the code (a separate instance, given the
  brief and the scripts). Changed on its findings, before any number: the three tests moved to the
  queries whose folder is not whole (H21a had been on all minority queries); the rows beside the
  tests lost their readings; near-ties are bounded; the recall tables gained owner-weighted columns;
  fsample is compared with usample; the hash seeds are reported in the restricted cells; the driver
  refuses other budgets and reports a registered run that did not finish.

## Hypotheses (fixed now, do not edit after the first evaluation of S19)
One family, three tests, under E1 on S19 at 2 KiB (S3's allowance for user metadata, half an ext4
block; under E1 eight one-bit vectors against the mean in int8), each read on its 98.33 percent
interval. All three are read on queries whose folder is not whole at 2 KiB, where what is stored is
a strict subset of the folder: a folder stored whole is every file at one bit under sample, kmeans
and usample alike, which session 20 already compared with the mean, and counting those queries
would pull the two differences between samples to zero.
- H21a, the bytes are better spent on a few coarse file vectors than on one precise mean: sample
  minus mean on the minority queries whose folder is not whole, read as session 19 reads a loss
  (confirmed if the lower bound is at least +0.05; below +0.05 if the upper bound is under it;
  present with the size open if the lower bound is above zero; inconclusive otherwise).
- H21b, the sample needs no clustering: sample minus kmeans over all queries whose folder is not
  whole. Not inferior if the lower bound is at least -0.02; inferior if the upper bound is under
  -0.02; inconclusive otherwise.
- H21c, the kinds: sample minus usample on the minority queries whose folder is not whole. The kinds
  matter if the lower bound is above zero; the uniform sample is better if the upper bound is below
  zero; no difference at the margin if the interval lies within -0.02 and +0.02; inconclusive
  otherwise.
Reported beside each test, without a reading: the same difference on the cell without the
restriction (all minority queries for H21a and H21c, all queries for H21b), and the test's own
difference with near-ties counted for the own folder and against it. A near-tie is a folder scoring
within 1e-6 of the query's own folder; the study's rank counts only folders scoring strictly higher,
which favours a summary made of files' own vectors when another folder holds a copy of the same
file, so both bounds are printed with the share of queries they move.
Reported without a reading: every policy at every budget, in every cell and size class, on both
sources and under every encoder run, owner-weighted on S19 in the cells the tests use; kmeans,
kkind, medoid, ffirst, allbits, kindmean and fsample against mean, sample, usample and one another
at equal bytes; the distance of sample to every file opened in f32; the share of folders stored
whole and the bytes each policy uses; the two samples and their difference under five hash seeds, in
the restricted cells too.
Expectation, written before the run and not a test: H21a confirmed; H21c the kinds matter, because a
uniform sample of eight from a folder of thirty usually misses its three images; H21b open at 2 KiB,
where a sample of a large folder covers its majority worse than centroids do; fsample below sample
in small folders, where it leaves slots empty.

## What each outcome will mean for the paper (fixed now)
- H21a confirmed or present: at equal bytes a handful of files at one bit keeps the minority files
  findable where the mean in its best format does not. The remedy is how the bytes are spent, and
  the paper gives the table by budget. Below +0.05 or inconclusive: the paper says the advantage at
  equal bytes was not shown on the second source.
- H21b not inferior: the summary the paper recommends is a sample of the files' own vectors at one
  bit, which needs no clustering. Inferior: the paper says what clustering buys at that budget and
  that a summary made of files' own vectors pays it.
- H21c the kinds matter: the remedy keeps "per kind" in its name, and a summary that merges up a
  tree has to be fsample, at the price its rows against sample and usample show. No difference: the
  paper says the labels are not needed and the uniform sample, which merges exactly, is the
  recommendation. The uniform sample better: reported as such.
- Inconclusive is reported as inconclusive. No row is promoted to a result after the fact.

## Steps, in order
1. This brief, scripts/budget.py, scripts/s21_selftest.py and the driver scripts/s19_followup.sh in
   one commit, with an OpenTimestamps proof of its id in prereg/, before session 19's first
   evaluation. scripts/s19_selftest.py keeps its synthetic set when S19_KEEP is set, nothing else in
   it changes.
2. The session 19 driver runs scripts/s19_followup.sh after its outputs are pushed: the self-test,
   then budget.py on S19 under E1 (the registered tests), S11 under E1, S19 under E4 and E2, S11
   under E4, E2 and E3. results/session21_outputs.md, verbatim, is pushed after the two E1 runs and
   again at the end.
3. results/session21.md (this hypothesis block copied, verdicts, reading) and NOTES.md.

## Inputs and budget
- Nothing is downloaded or embedded. About ten minutes a run on the pod's three cores, seven runs,
  under $1 of the balance already there.

## Rules
- Nothing is chosen on the new corpus: budgets, policies, the registered budget and the readings are
  fixed above. A run that fails its reproduction check gives no numbers.
- One commit per deliverable. No em dashes.

