# dirvec, session 22: a folder's summary written to a real file system and read back

Why this session exists. All three reviewers of 4 October wrote that the setting is never built: "no
vector was written to a file system". One of them named the smallest experiment: lay the set out on
ext4, write the summary to an attribute, and count what a reader fetches against per-file
attributes, a sidecar file and a central index, with the cost of a change; the adviser added that
blocks should be counted and not milliseconds on a shared machine, and that the paper must say where
a central index wins. This session does that. It measures the store and nothing else: no vector is
computed, no folder is ranked, and no hypothesis of the study is read on it.

## Protocol (fixed now, before the real trees are laid out)
- Trees: the first 300 repositories in the order of data/ghdirs_s19.jsonl.gz (session 19's list of
  every eligible directory of every repository it read) that hold 5 to 400 eligible directories. A
  tree is those directories with their paths, their ancestors, and as many files in each as it has
  kept files. Every directory, eligible or an ancestor, gets a summary of B bytes; the bytes are
  random, since their values do not change what is read. Every file is one block of random data.
- Where a summary can live: an extended attribute of the directory (attr); a file inside the
  directory (sidecar); one index file at the root of each tree, a table of paths and offsets
  followed by the summaries (tree); one such file for the whole volume (global); and no folder
  summary at all, each file carrying its own 256-byte vector in an attribute and the reader fetching
  the vectors of a directory's files (files, on 256-byte and on 1,024-byte inodes).
- Budgets: 2,048 bytes; 4,000 bytes, about what one ext4 attribute block holds; 8,192 bytes, which
  ext4 stores as an attribute only with its ea_inode feature. One ext4 image per layout and budget
  (mke2fs defaults otherwise, 4 KiB blocks), on a loop device.
- Counting: the sectors the kernel reads from and writes to the image (/sys/block/<loop>/stat), in
  blocks of 4 KiB, after an unmount and a mount before every measurement. Reported twice: with
  readahead off (inode_readahead_blks=0 and read_ahead_kb=0), so that only the blocks asked for are
  counted, and with the kernel's readahead as shipped.
- What is measured: a scan (every directory of every tree visited and its summary fetched); 400
  descents (a tree and one of its eligible directories drawn with seed 20261105; the reader starts
  at the tree's root, lists the directory, fetches the summary of every child directory, steps into
  the child on the way and repeats until the target is listed), each beside the same walk without
  the summaries, the difference being what the summaries cost; 40 updates (one directory's summary
  replaced: blocks written by sync and by unmount); and whether a directory's attribute survives
  cp, tar, git and mv.
- Reported as measured, on one kernel and one file system: the table per layout and budget. The
  paper says in which layout the reader fetches the fewest blocks, including where that is the
  packed index and not the attribute.

## Steps
1. This section and scripts/store.py (tested on a synthetic list of 13 trees) in one commit.
2. One run for each readahead setting in the workspace (root, loop mounts; no pod, no cost) once the
   session 19 pod has pushed data/ghdirs_s19.jsonl.gz: results/session22_outputs.md, verbatim.
3. results/session22.md and NOTES.md.

---

# dirvec, session 23: a walk down whole repository trees, guided by folder summaries

Why this session exists. The reviewers of 4 October asked for real nesting with vectors at inner
nodes: whole trees, a descent within a repository, the directory's own files apart from its subtree,
names as a baseline that vectors must add to, and the cost counted in what is read. It is also the
first form of the idea this study came from: a summary on the folder that says what is in the box,
so that a reader picks the right directory to walk down whatever the folder is called. Session 19
scores single directories, at most four from a repository, and its list of every eligible directory
shows where the kinds mix in repositories: 1,501 of 35,970 eligible directories hold an image beside
another kept file. A leaf is mostly one kind; the mixing is above the leaves. This session takes
repositories whole and walks them.

When this was fixed. Committed on 5 October 2026 while the session 19 pod was embedding its files
under the first encoder: no retrieval number of the new source existed. Seen by then: session 19's
corpus counts (12,656 files in 1,600 directories of 1,079 repositories, 1,053 owners; the directory
list above), session 22's block counts, and an estimate from session 19's list that about 7 percent
of the repositories read would qualify as trees here (314 of 4,320, counted on eligible directories).

## Definitions (fixed now)
- Trees (scripts/tree_fetch.py). The pool is session 19's (5,517 usable repositories of 14 days),
  read in a seeded order of its own (seed 20261106, repositories that session 19 read included). A
  repository is taken whole when at least 8 of its listed directories hold a kept file, one of them
  at depth 2 or more, with 40 to 300 kept files, at most 200 MB of them, and at least 5 kept files
  with an image extension and 5 others. Listed directories, kept files and input rules are session
  19's. Reading stops at 200 trees or 22,000 files. This set is selected for trees that hold both
  input groups; every repository read is logged with its counts and the first rule it failed, and
  the share that qualifies is reported. The rule counts image extensions, while the cells below use
  image inputs, which also take in a PDF without a text layer.
- Queries: build_gt.py's leave-one-out queries (a file in a directory of at least three files,
  near-duplicates left out) that have a vector and whose directory holds another embedded file. The
  target is the query's directory. Encoder E1.
- Summaries. Every directory of a tree, with or without files of its own, has a summary of its
  subtree; a directory with files has one of its own files too. The query is taken out of every
  summary that held it. Policies, as scripts/budget.py defines them (hash seed 21): mean, usample,
  fsample, sample, and all (every file at one bit, no budget). For usample, and for fsample at
  these budgets (n is at least the six kinds), a directory's subtree summary is exactly what
  merging its own-files summary with its children's subtree summaries gives, so the summaries
  scored here are the ones a tree maintained by merging would hold (a stored vector then carries
  its priority and kind, a few bytes not counted). fsample leaves a slot empty when a kind has
  fewer files than its share, so it stores fewer vectors than usample; the vectors stored per
  summary are reported for every policy. Two rows use no vector: names, the largest cosine between
  the query file's whole lowercased name, extension included, and the names of a candidate's files
  (character 3- and 4-grams, TF-IDF fitted on the names of the repository's embedded files), which
  is what a listing of unlimited length would allow; and chance, a reader picking a candidate at
  random at every step.
- The walk (scripts/tree.py). The reader starts at the repository's root. At a directory it scores
  "stay" by the directory's own-files summary and each child by the child's subtree summary (the
  largest cosine between the float32 query and what is stored), takes the best, the first of equal
  scores with "stay" first, and stops when "stay" wins or nothing is left to score. Success: it
  stops in the target. Cost: one summary read per candidate scored. Flat: the own-files summary of
  every directory of the repository is read and the best taken, the first of equal scores in the
  order of depth and path, as in the walk; success when the target is first. Queries whose walk or
  flat rank turns on a rival within 1e-6 are counted.
- Budgets 2 KiB and 8 KiB per summary (8 and 32 one-bit vectors under E1; the mean in int8 and in
  f32). The tests are read at 2 KiB.
- Cells. all; minority: the query's input group (image inputs against the rest) holds under half of
  the repository's other embedded files and the query's directory holds a sibling of its group.
- Estimator: session 19's. The mean over owners of the owner's mean, percentile intervals from
  10,000 resamples of owners; a test with fewer than 100 owners is inconclusive.
- scripts/s23_selftest.py recomputes every walk, its first step, the summaries it read and the flat
  rank with a second, plain implementation on synthetic trees, and must pass on the pod first.
- Read before the commit by a reviewer instance that had not seen the code. Changed on its findings,
  before any number: flat breaks ties by position as the walk does (it had counted a tie with the
  target as a hit); names use the whole file name with its extension; the vectors each policy
  stores, the reads owner-weighted and the near-ties are reported; a row for sample against usample
  separates the kinds from fsample's empty slots; a run that stops pushes its log.

## Hypotheses (fixed now, do not edit after the first evaluation)
One family, three tests, under E1 at 2 KiB, each read on its 98.33 percent interval:
- H23a, the loss at inner nodes: walk success with fsample minus walk success with mean on the
  minority queries, read as session 19 reads a loss (confirmed if the lower bound is at least +0.05;
  below +0.05 if the upper bound is under it; present with the size open if the lower bound is above
  zero; inconclusive otherwise).
- H23b, the kinds in a summary that merges: walk success with fsample minus with usample on the
  minority queries. The kinds matter if the lower bound is above zero; the uniform sample is better
  if the upper bound is below zero; no difference at the margin if the interval lies within -0.02
  and +0.02; inconclusive otherwise.
- H23c, the walk against reading every directory: walk success minus flat success, both with
  fsample, over all queries. Not inferior if the lower bound is at least -0.02; inferior if the upper
  bound is under -0.02; inconclusive otherwise. Reported with the summaries each reads.
A reading of "below +0.05" whose interval still lies above zero is reported as an advantage smaller
than the registered size, not as none; the same holds for H21a and H19a.
Reported without a reading: the same three at 8 KiB; names and chance; sample and all; sample
against usample, which is the kinds with every slot used; the first step; the walk by the depth of
the target; the cell that is not minority; flat for every summary.
Expectation, written before the run and not a test: H23a confirmed, since an image query in a
repository of mostly text meets children whose means all look like text; H23b the kinds matter;
H23c open, since a walk compounds its errors over the levels; names strong, since session 17 found
file-name routers level with the pooled vector on leave-one-out queries.

## What each outcome will mean for the paper (fixed now)
- H23a confirmed or present: the loss is shown where repositories mix their kinds, at inner nodes,
  with summaries that merge up the tree, and the setting of the paper is measured on trees. Below
  +0.05 or inconclusive: the paper reports the walk as built and says that a per-kind summary did
  not help it at the registered size.
- H23b the kinds matter: a summary that merges needs its share per kind. No difference: the uniform
  sample is enough for the walk. The uniform sample better: read with the vectors each stores and
  with the row for sample against usample, since fsample's empty slots and not the kinds may be the
  reason.
- H23c not inferior: a reader finds the directory after reading a fraction of the summaries, and
  the paper gives the fraction. Inferior: the paper says so and gives both costs; the summaries then
  serve a flat read of a tree and not a descent.
- If names beat fsample over all queries, the paper says that on leave-one-out queries in
  repositories the file names are the stronger signal, and reports where the vectors add to them.
- Inconclusive is reported as inconclusive.

## Steps, in order
1. This brief, scripts/tree_fetch.py, scripts/tree.py, scripts/s23_selftest.py, scripts/s23_run.sh
   and the call at the end of scripts/s19_followup.sh in one commit, with an OpenTimestamps proof
   of its id, before session 19's first evaluation.
2. On the pod after session 21: the self-test, the harvest, manifest and ground truth (pushed before
   anything is embedded), E1 embedding, tree.py. results/session23_outputs.md, verbatim.
3. results/session23.md and NOTES.md.

## Inputs and budget
- About 22,000 files to embed under E1: about three and a half hours on the slice, about $2 of the
  balance already there. The watcher's deadline (16:41 UTC) stands.

## Rules
- Nothing is chosen on the trees: rules, budgets, summaries and readings are fixed above.
- One commit per deliverable. No em dashes.

